  <h2>What is Shizuku?</h2>
  <p>
    Shizuku — Japanese for "droplet", hence the water-drop icon — is an open-source
    tool developed by RikkaApps. It starts a small service process with ADB-level
    privileges, and apps written for Shizuku then call system APIs through that
    service: things stock Android never lets an ordinary app do, achieved without
    rooting the device.
  </p>

  <h2>How it starts</h2>
  <p>
    By running a single <a href="/adb">ADB</a> shell command from a computer — or,
    on Android 11 and later, straight from the phone itself via wireless debugging.
    Devices that are already rooted can start the service directly. The service
    stops when the device reboots and has to be started again.
  </p>

  <h2>Where Root My Android fits</h2>
  <p>
    ADB is exactly what our tooling speaks: the free
    <a href="/scan">browser-based scan</a> talks to your device over USB with the
    same protocol Shizuku uses to start. Run it to confirm your device is reachable
    and see what state it's in.
  </p>
